1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,920
An AI just cracked a million-dollar
maths problem… in 88 hours.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,170
So why is nobody paying out?

3
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:09,810
Every drop of water follows a rulebook.

4
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,620
Written in the 1800s, it
predicts how water and air move:

5
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:17,020
weather, plane wings, even
the blood in your veins.

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:20,100
Mathematicians call it the
Navier-Stokes equations.

7
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:22,450
But one question was never answered.

8
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:24,440
Can that rulebook break?

9
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:28,750
Could a smooth swirl of water
ever spin so tight… that one tiny

10
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,030
spot moves infinitely fast?

11
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:35,360
In 2000, a maths institute put
a million dollars on the answer.

12
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,430
Then OpenAI sent in
10,000 copies of its AI.

13
00:00:39,430 --> 00:00:41,780
88 hours later: yes.

14
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:43,110
It can break.

15
00:00:43,110 --> 00:00:47,620
A whirlpool spirals inward, its
core shrinking and spinning faster…

16
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:50,690
and faster… until, at one single point,

17
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:52,750
the speed hits infinity.

18
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,830
Will your bath tap explode?

19
00:00:54,830 --> 00:00:55,430
No.

20
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:57,460
Real water is made of molecules.

21
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,620
This is about whether the maths holds up.

22
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:01,100
But here's the catch.

23
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:04,200
In the AI's version, the
water is never left alone.

24
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,170
It gets a gentle, smooth
push the whole time,

25
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:08,500
like stirring your tea.

26
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,230
The prize rules allow that.

27
00:01:10,230 --> 00:01:14,450
But many mathematicians say the
real mystery is water left alone.

28
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:16,860
So who checks a proof this hard?

29
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:18,970
A computer referee called Lean.

30
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:23,370
It reads every step and only says
yes if each one follows the rules.

31
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:24,640
It said yes.

32
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:28,230
But a referee checks the moves…
not whether it's the right game.

33
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:32,120
Humans still have to confirm the
AI answered the right question.

34
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,940
And this month, mathematicians said OpenAI
had described its own method wrong.

35
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:40,300
The man who wrote the official problem
calls two human mathematicians

36
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,620
the real heroes.

37
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,340
Their work paved the way.

38
00:01:43,530 --> 00:01:44,870
And the million dollars?

39
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:48,420
The computing reportedly cost
more than that… and OpenAI says

40
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:49,930
it won't even claim it.

41
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:53,490
Funny thing: the only other
Millennium problem ever solved?

42
00:01:53,490 --> 00:01:55,650
Its winner turned the million down too.

43
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:57,690
The prize committee is still checking.

44
00:01:57,690 --> 00:01:58,590
Slowly.

45
00:01:58,590 --> 00:01:59,590
Carefully.

46
00:01:59,590 --> 00:02:02,840
So did an AI just break
the rulebook of water?

47
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:03,610
Maybe.

48
00:02:03,610 --> 00:02:06,930
Either way, machines just cracked
open a mystery humans chased

49
00:02:06,930 --> 00:02:08,480
for nearly a century.

50
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:11,460
Follow for more AI, explained like this.
